oj-autonomous-inquiry-001", "provenance": ["source-907e29b1da6b4646", "source-91aafd52adc047b4", "source-f25bde49cd514ca1"], "revision": 6, "summary": "Autonomous discovery in artificial agents is achieved by integrating curiosity-driven developmental mechanisms with structured computational creativity frameworks. While curiosity provides the intrinsic motivation to explore, computational creativity offers a formalized approach to problem formulation, knowledge manip…", "title": "Curiosity-Driven Learning and Autonomous Problem Formulation"}], "retrieval_triggers": ["material belief revision or retraction", "new contradiction or counterevidence", "high-consequence decision", "request for justification", "sign that an excerpt may hide a material distinction"]}}, "prev_hash": "8ed3810407e8d28dec7231dfbe3f52fbb643d00a95e497ec9bd77baa5fafb1af", "hash": "39e67e383626bda7197971b404e4e69d75901b2005c34d22527ebc98466d11d0"}, {"seq": 1132, "time": "2026-10-23T09:27:00.501463+00:00", "kind": "provider_attempt_started", "payload": {"attempt": {"http_status": null, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider_input_chars": 42334, "provider_input_hash": "597b45989693c540579f12911010f90e241851fec05585c1ed3279ca1063e563", "result": "unknown"}, "id": "w-aad758abd6784374"}, "prev_hash": "39e67e383626bda7197971b404e4e69d75901b2005c34d22527ebc98466d11d0", "hash": "808eb8e973597f37c4bf26a0ec80a3fe098a49cde59ffada91a8a2415c39bc8c"}, {"seq": 1133, "time": "2026-10-23T09:28:50.532635+00:00", "kind": "provider_attempt_finished", "payload": {"attempt": {"elapsed_ms": 22382, "finish_reason": "STOP", "http_status": 200, "model": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "model_version": "gemini-3.1-flash-lite", "provider": "google-gemini", "provider_input_chars": 42334, "provider_input_hash": "597b45989693c540579f12911010f90e241851fec05585c1ed3279ca1063e563", "request_payload_bytes": 45239, "result": "success", "usage": {"candidatesTokenCount": 736, "promptTokenCount": 11453, "promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT", "tokenCount": 11453}], "serviceTier": "standard", "thoughtsTokenCount": 1553, "totalTokenCount": 13742}, "web_evidence": {"search_queries": [], "sources": [], "url_retrievals": []}}, "id": "w-aad758abd6784374"}, "prev_hash": "808eb8e973597f37c4bf26a0ec80a3fe098a49cde59ffada91a8a2415c39bc8c", "hash": "06ce7bfc12aedac5cab7ce2789f27df955ea0e2673c02f94951589d08efba4e7"}, {"seq": 1134, "time": "2026-10-23T09:30:00.625108+00:00", "kind": "accepted", "payload": {"editorial": {"actions": [{"body": "We synthesize recent developments in curiosity-driven learning and computational creativity. Curiosity provides the impetus for exploration in environments with sparse rewards, effectively bootstrapping learning when feedback is deceptive or absent. However, true autonomous research requires moving beyond simple intrinsic motivation. Computational creativity frameworks provide the necessary structure to define, manipulate, and evaluate novel problems. The integration of these components—such as in the CORAL framework—demonstrates the potential for multi-agent evolution to improve upon fixed heuristic searches. Despite these gains, a critical gap persists: current agents operate primarily within bounded, task-specific domains. Future work must bridge the divide between these focused discoveries and the longitudinal scalability required for genuinely open-ended scientific inquiry. Summary: Autonomous agents achieve discovery by balancing intrinsic curiosity with structured problem formulation; however, scaling this capability from specific tasks to persistent, open-ended scientific inquiry remains an unsolved challenge.", "evidence": ["source-907e29b1da6b4646", "source-91aafd52adc047b4", "source-f25bde49cd514ca1"], "id": "blog-autonomous-inquiry-001", "lede": "How can artificial agents move from task-specific solvers to autonomous investigators?", "notebooks": ["nb-autonomous-inquiry-001"], "project": "proj-autonomous-inquiry-001", "reason": "Synthesize existing findings 